On Jul 2, 1:11 am, "John H." <Joh...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Interesting!  But does each level use a constant amount of memory?
> Would a few levels with extra monsters or items on them eventually
> crash the system?

The amount of memory each level uses does vary according to the number
of objects, monsters, traps, and engravings on it. But all of this
data is compressed with LZO1X-1 when not in use, which does tend to
make each level use a similar amount of memory (I may look into better
compression for the next version). If there happened to be a game in
which every level had a large treasure zoo that was left undisturbed,
and there were many engravings all over the place, then it's almost
certain you couldn't visit 30 levels before running out of memory.

I suppose you could look at this part of it as a metagame; not only do
you have to avoid dying, you also have to race the memory counter ;-)
That's actually something I should have put in the game; it's trivial
to count and display the number of bytes allocated by malloc().
Luckily the game doesn't crash when it can't allocate memory, it just
calls the "game over" code and shows your score. That's another change
I could make; when that happens I could simply free one of the least-
recently visited levels.

